Hello Fabbaloo team,

a release lead that may fit your coverage of the software side of AM.

Solidon3D sits between the downloaded model and the slicer. Click a hole and
its dimensions appear on the model, type 8 instead of 6 and the countersink
follows, pick a fit for the screw, read the check report, send the file to the
slicer you already use. No CAD knowledge required. Until now the result of an
edit was always a triangle mesh: useful for printing, useless for anyone who
wanted to carry the part into a CAD system.

Version 0.5.0 changes that. Holes, countersinks and threads stay real faces
and edges when they are moved, duplicated or removed, and STEP goes in and
comes back out cleanly. Two geometry kernels now run under one interface, and
the options that used to switch between them have been removed. Solidon keeps
the exact body as long as it can and says so when it cannot; existing projects
keep computing unchanged.

The reason this matters is the shape of the current market. CAD packages
accept an STL but their tools do not grip triangles, so the part gets rebuilt.
Mesh tools edit triangles and never hand back exact surfaces, and the two best
known ones, Meshmixer and Microsoft's 3D Builder, have been discontinued for
years. Parametric customisers only work when the original author anticipated
the parameter. The most common job in printing, adapting a downloaded part so
it fits something, has been without a proper tool.

Worth noting is what is not claimed. Solidon does not reconstruct a CAD model
from arbitrary triangles. It recognises features, carries them forward exactly
where that is possible, and names the limit where it is not. Every dimension
states in the feature window whether it was measured on the mesh, fitted to a
surface, or taken from a construction step.

The second thing that separates Solidon from its neighbours matters more day
to day: the check report gives the reason. Not "warning: overhang", but which
island in which layer will cost support material and how far the part would
have to be turned. A slicer measures this too, but only once the part is
finished. The same idea runs through clearances, which are entered as a
reference to the material rather than as a number, with profiles you can
calibrate to your own printer by printing and measuring the supplied test
pieces.

The demo is free and complete, so nothing is needed from me to try it. Images
in print resolution are available on request.

Solidon3D is an independent development from Germany, for Windows, macOS and
Linux, in six languages, with no account and no network.

